Before: `OrderService` directly constructs and depends on a concrete database class.

```java
class OrderService {
    private MySqlOrderRepository repo = new MySqlOrderRepository();
    void placeOrder(Order o) { repo.save(o); }
}
```

After: `OrderService` depends only on an interface it owns; the concrete class is supplied from outside.

```java
interface OrderRepository {
    void save(Order o);
}
class MySqlOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    public void save(Order o) { /* SQL insert */ }
}
class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repo;
    OrderService(OrderRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
    void placeOrder(Order o) { repo.save(o); }
}
```

`OrderService` can now be tested with an in-memory fake `OrderRepository`, and swapping to Postgres means writing a new class — zero edits to `OrderService`.
